.planning/ROADMAP.md:30:- [ ] **Phase 26: Migration, Export, Backup, and Restore** - Make state portable, quarantined, reversible, and secret-safe.
.planning/ROADMAP.md:135:### Phase 26: Migration, Export, Backup, and Restore
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:235:    - Every one of those nine judgements is ALSO emitted as a machine-parseable status line, because a certification is a document its own author wrote and a gate that merely counts its words is a tautology. The grammar is fixed and the gate enforces it: exactly one line per key, `F26-SC1:`/`F26-SC2:`/`F26-SC3:`/`F26-SC4:` for the criteria and `F26-01:`…`F26-05:` for the requirements, each followed by exactly one of `CLOSED`, `LINUX-ONLY` or `OPEN`, then an em-dash and at least twenty characters of substance. Every `CLOSED` line additionally carries `platform=` naming `linux`, `windows` and/or `macos`, and `evidence=` naming something re-runnable. `F26-SC3` and `F26-SC4` cannot be `CLOSED` without `windows` among their platforms — Linux is the easy platform and those two exist precisely to cross it.
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:236:    - The certification names the tree it certifies on a single line `F26-CERT-SHA: <40-hex>`, and that SHA is a real commit that is an ancestor of this branch's head — so the evidence can be re-run against a known tree rather than an unstated one.
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:245:Emit the nine machine-parseable status lines the behaviors specify, plus the `F26-CERT-SHA` line, and treat that grammar as a floor rather than the whole job: the prose around each line is what a human reads, and the line is what makes the claim checkable. The grammar exists because every earlier draft of this gate counted lines in a file this very task writes, which is a tautology — an executor could satisfy it by typing sixty lines of anything. A malformed line, a missing key, a duplicated key, a `CLOSED` with no named platform or no named evidence, or `F26-SC3`/`F26-SC4` claimed `CLOSED` without Windows all go red.
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:264:    <automated>cd /Users/seandonahoe/dev/waylandcore-ferrox &amp;&amp; C=.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-CERTIFICATION.md; test "$(/usr/bin/grep -cF 'wcore-protocol' "$C")" -ge "1" || { echo "GATE: the deliberately uncrossed wcore-protocol seam is not recorded"; exit 1; }; CS=$(/usr/bin/grep -oE '^F26-CERT-SHA: [0-9a-f]{40}$' "$C" | /usr/bin/sed 's/^F26-CERT-SHA: //'); test -n "$CS" || { echo "GATE: no 'F26-CERT-SHA: <40-hex>' line - the certification names no tree its evidence can be re-run against"; exit 1; }; /usr/bin/git cat-file -e "$CS^{commit}" 2&gt;/dev/null || { echo "GATE: F26-CERT-SHA $CS is not a commit in this repository"; exit 1; }; /usr/bin/git merge-base --is-ancestor "$CS" HEAD || { echo "GATE: F26-CERT-SHA $CS is not an ancestor of HEAD - the certification names a tree this branch never had"; exit 1; }; echo "certified_sha=$CS"</automated>
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:274:  <read_first>.planning/ROADMAP.md — the Phase 26 section, because the four Success Criteria in their AUTHORITATIVE wording are what is being judged and the gate below extracts that block mechanically rather than letting anyone retype it, .planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-CERTIFICATION.md as Task 3 wrote it (the nine status lines and the `F26-CERT-SHA` line this task replays against), .planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-01-BASELINE.md (the `F26-SC1-MACOS:` marker specifically — its CI run id and head SHA are re-derived from GitHub here, because that leg is the one an earlier revision of this phase tried to certify around), scripts/panel-ask.sh and scripts/panel-decision-check.sh (the phase's panel harness, built and self-tested in 26-01 — this task CALLS it and does not rebuild it)</read_first>
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:317:**STEP 1 — REPLAY EVERY CLOSED CLAIM, DO NOT READ IT.** Build `scripts/portability-evidence-replay.sh`, taking the certification path and an output directory. It parses the nine status lines, takes the `F26-CERT-SHA`, and for every key marked `CLOSED` it RE-EXECUTES that key's named evidence at that SHA and prints its own verdict line: `REPLAY: <key> evidence=<path> host=<host> result=reproduced|failed|not-replayable reason=<text>`.
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:351:cd /Users/seandonahoe/dev/waylandcore-ferrox &amp;&amp; D=.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/panel/26-04-phase-acceptance; R="$D/roadmap-criteria.txt"; test -s "$R" || { echo "GATE: no verbatim ROADMAP criteria extract at $R - the panel had only the certification's restatement to judge against, which is the substitution this plan's own review list warns about first"; exit 1; }; test "$(/usr/bin/grep -c . "$R")" -ge 4 || { echo "GATE: the extract is too small to hold four Success Criteria"; exit 1; }; /usr/bin/awk '/^### Phase 26:/{f=1} f&amp;&amp;/^### Phase 27:/{f=0} f' .planning/ROADMAP.md &gt; /tmp/26-04-roadmap-phase26.txt; test -s /tmp/26-04-roadmap-phase26.txt || { echo "GATE: could not locate the Phase 26 section in ROADMAP.md"; exit 1; }; MISS=0; while IFS= read -r l; do test -n "$l" || continue; /usr/bin/grep -qF -- "$l" /tmp/26-04-roadmap-phase26.txt || { echo "NOT PRESENT VERBATIM IN ROADMAP.md: $l"; MISS=$((MISS+1)); }; done &lt; "$R"; echo "extract_lines=$(/usr/bin/grep -c . "$R") not_verbatim=$MISS"; test "$MISS" -eq 0 || { echo "GATE: the criteria block the panel was shown is not byte-for-byte what ROADMAP.md says. A criterion restated more loosely is a criterion quietly lowered, and that is the substitution this gate exists to make impossible"; exit 1; }
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:367:cd /Users/seandonahoe/dev/waylandcore-ferrox &amp;&amp; P=.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore; CERT="$P/26-04-CERTIFICATION.md"; BASE="$P/26-01-BASELINE.md"; test -s "$CERT" || { echo "GATE: no certification"; exit 1; }; if /usr/bin/grep -qE '^F26-(SC1|01): CLOSED — ' "$CERT"; then test -s "$BASE" || { echo "GATE: F26-SC1 or F26-01 is CLOSED but 26-01-BASELINE.md does not exist to carry the macOS marker"; exit 1; }; M=$(/usr/bin/grep -oE 'F26-SC1-MACOS: RAN — run=[0-9]+ sha=[0-9a-f]{40} binary=[^[:space:]]+ arch=arm64 hermes_profiles=12 openclaw_items=[1-9][0-9]* secret_hits=0' "$BASE" | /usr/bin/head -1); test -n "$M" || { echo "GATE: F26-SC1 or F26-01 is certified CLOSED but 26-01 records no well-formed RAN marker for the mandatory macOS real-state leg. That leg is MANDATORY and its binary is obtainable from CI's build job, so a missing leg is unfinished work - not an environmental limit, and not a criterion to certify around"; exit 1; }; echo "$M"; /usr/bin/grep -E '^F26-SC1: CLOSED — ' "$CERT" | /usr/bin/grep -q 'macos' || { echo "GATE: F26-SC1 is CLOSED without macos among its platforms, while the leg that closes it ran only there"; exit 1; }; RUN=$(echo "$M" | /usr/bin/sed -E 's/.* run=([0-9]+) .*/\1/'); SHA=$(echo "$M" | /usr/bin/sed -E 's/.* sha=([0-9a-f]{40}) .*/\1/'); command -v gh &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 || { echo "GATE: gh is unavailable, so the macOS artifact's provenance cannot be re-derived and the marker is only a claim"; exit 1; }; HS=$(gh run view "$RUN" -R FerroxLabs/wayland-core --json headSha --jq .headSha 2&gt;/dev/null); test "$HS" = "$SHA" || { echo "GATE: run $RUN reports headSha '$HS', not the recorded $SHA"; exit 1; }; gh run view "$RUN" -R FerroxLabs/wayland-core --json jobs --jq '.jobs[] | select(.name=="Build (aarch64-apple-darwin)") | .conclusion' 2&gt;/dev/null &gt; /tmp/26-04-t4-job.txt; /usr/bin/grep -qx 'success' /tmp/26-04-t4-job.txt || { echo "GATE: run $RUN has no successful Build (aarch64-apple-darwin) job"; exit 1; }; gh api "repos/FerroxLabs/wayland-core/actions/runs/$RUN/artifacts" --jq '.artifacts[] | select(.expired==false and .size_in_bytes&gt;0) | .name' 2&gt;/dev/null &gt; /tmp/26-04-t4-art.txt; /usr/bin/grep -qx 'wayland-core-aarch64-apple-darwin' /tmp/26-04-t4-art.txt || { echo "GATE: run $RUN publishes no LIVE non-empty aarch64 macOS artifact"; exit 1; }; echo "macOS leg re-derived from GitHub: run=$RUN sha=$SHA"; else echo "F26-SC1 and F26-01 are not certified CLOSED; the macOS provenance re-derivation does not apply and the open clause is checked by the certification gates in Task 3"; fi
.planning/phases/26-migration-export-backup-restore/26-04-PLAN.md:423:Local gates (Mac, source level only): `cargo fmt --all -- --check` clean; the hostile generator is valid Python; the hostile suite demonstrably spawns the real binary; both matrix scripts exist, the shell one parses, and each is PROVEN able to go red by being handed a binary path that does not exist and required to exit non-zero — an existence check alone passes on an empty file, and `sh -n` passes on an empty file too, so neither is evidence about the script that carries this plan's whole cross-platform claim. The certification is gated on GRAMMAR rather than on length: exactly one conforming status line per criterion and per requirement, every `CLOSED` naming a platform and re-runnable evidence, `F26-SC3` and `F26-SC4` refused as `CLOSED` without Windows, the `wcore-protocol` non-change recorded, and a `F26-CERT-SHA` that is a real commit ancestral to this branch's head. Counting lines in a file this plan itself writes is a tautology and is not used.
